I don't know if it's
going to happen or not

00:19:29.440 --> 00:19:31.545
but the papers are saying
he may go bankrupt.

00:19:31.545 --> 00:19:34.553
He made three quarters of a
billion dollars as a movie star

00:19:34.553 --> 00:19:36.693
but he spent $30,000 a month on wine.

00:19:36.693 --> 00:19:38.232
He must be feeling good

00:19:38.232 --> 00:19:41.742
and secondly he took Hunter
Thompson, the reporter,

00:19:41.742 --> 00:19:43.017
he burned his body

00:19:43.017 --> 00:19:44.351
and paid three million dollars

00:19:44.351 --> 00:19:47.398
to blow his ashes into
the sky in a cannon.

00:19:47.398 --> 00:19:48.676
So no matter how much money you make,

00:19:48.676 --> 00:19:49.878
you can screw it up, right?

00:19:49.878 --> 00:19:51.902
But if you will just decide,

00:19:51.902 --> 00:19:55.315
there's a percentage of my
income that no matter what

00:19:55.315 --> 00:19:56.447
I'm going to have it automated,

00:19:56.447 --> 00:19:57.280
I'm not going to see it.

00:19:57.280 --> 00:19:58.831
It's going to go straight
to an investment account.

00:19:58.831 --> 00:20:01.423
And I don't know if it's 10 or 15 or 20.

00:20:01.423 --> 00:20:03.474
Those numbers sound
gigantic to most people.

00:20:03.474 --> 00:20:05.606
Even if you start with five and build

00:20:05.606 --> 00:20:07.562
that income does not go to anybody else

00:20:07.562 --> 00:20:09.082
and then you put that into compounding.

00:20:09.082 --> 00:20:10.495
So listen to this.

00:20:10.495 --> 00:20:12.279
There's a young man, true story,

00:20:12.279 --> 00:20:15.489
a Theodore Johnson, 1950s, worked for UPS.

00:20:15.489 --> 00:20:19.194
Never made more than
$14,000 in annual income

00:20:19.194 --> 00:20:20.227
in his entire life,

00:20:20.227 --> 00:20:24.892
retired with $70 million and
he didn't inherit a dime.

00:20:24.892 --> 00:20:27.095
He gave away 35 million
while he was alive.

00:20:27.095 --> 00:20:28.561
Now how is that possible?

00:20:28.561 --> 00:20:30.926
Because of compound interest.

00:20:30.926 --> 00:20:32.557
I interview all these investors

00:20:32.557 --> 00:20:34.558
and say what's the biggest
mistake Americans make

00:20:34.558 --> 00:20:35.966
he said, they all said

00:20:35.966 --> 00:20:38.043
they don't tap into compound interest.

00:20:38.043 --> 00:20:39.549
They know intellectually
a little bit what it means

00:20:39.549 --> 00:20:40.428
but they don't do it,

00:20:40.428 --> 00:20:41.845
they don't do it consistently.

00:20:41.845 --> 00:20:42.770
So what do you do?

00:20:42.770 --> 00:20:43.619
- It basically just means

00:20:43.619 --> 00:20:44.452
that the profits you made

00:20:44.452 --> 00:20:45.836
could go back into the investment.

00:20:45.836 --> 00:20:46.872
- You keep reinvesting
and it keeps growing

00:20:46.872 --> 00:20:48.529
but it grows geometrically, right?

00:20:48.529 --> 00:20:51.812
And so a friend of his came
to this man who worked for UPS

00:20:51.812 --> 00:20:53.400
and said I'm going to make you rich.

00:20:53.400 --> 00:20:55.603
We're going to put a 20% tax on you,

00:20:55.603 --> 00:20:57.215
he said I can't give up 20%.

00:20:57.215 --> 00:20:58.369
I can't pay my bills if I do that.

00:20:58.369 --> 00:20:59.314
He said listen,

00:20:59.314 --> 00:21:01.400
if the government raise your taxes 20%

00:21:01.400 --> 00:21:04.197
and you'd claim, you'd
screamed, you'd cry,

00:21:04.197 --> 00:21:05.578
you'd complain and you'd pay it

00:21:05.578 --> 00:21:06.680
and you get adjusted to it

00:21:06.680 --> 00:21:07.911
but he said this is not
going to the government,

00:21:07.911 --> 00:21:10.076
this is going into an
account for your future

00:21:10.076 --> 00:21:13.292
and that compounding is what
made him that much money.

00:21:13.292 --> 00:21:16.903
In the book, I give you an
example of an 18-year-old boy

00:21:16.903 --> 00:21:19.198
whose father convinces
him that he should save

00:21:19.198 --> 00:21:21.084
$300 a month out of his income.

00:21:21.084 --> 00:21:23.141
not a huge income that sounds like a lot.

00:21:23.141 --> 00:21:24.040
But when you automate it,

00:21:24.040 --> 00:21:25.201
you forget about it, it's perfect.

00:21:25.201 --> 00:21:26.034
You adjust.

00:21:26.034 --> 00:21:30.534
Well he does this from 18 years
old to 27 for eight years.

00:21:30.534 --> 00:21:32.823
So the total amount of
money $300 a month's

00:21:32.823 --> 00:21:36.331
about 4,000 a year, that's $28,000 total.

00:21:36.331 --> 00:21:37.527
He didn't put, he leaves it in the market

00:21:37.527 --> 00:21:41.392
and never adds a dime to
it from 28 years old on.

00:21:41.392 --> 00:21:45.869
At 65, because the market
over those 30 years grew 10%

00:21:45.869 --> 00:21:49.634
his money has compounded so
much he has $1.8 million.

00:21:49.634 --> 00:21:51.357
You don't need a lot
of money to be wealthy.

00:21:51.357 --> 00:21:52.190
You need time.

00:21:52.190 --> 00:21:54.517
- So you're saying that
even if I have student debt,

00:21:54.517 --> 00:21:55.817
that I'm struggling to pay off.

00:21:55.817 --> 00:21:58.824
Even if I'm making say $1,000 a week,

00:21:58.824 --> 00:22:01.244
that I should still
try to put a percentage

00:22:01.244 --> 00:22:03.035
or make it a point to put a percentage

00:22:03.035 --> 00:22:04.698
not just into a savings account

00:22:04.698 --> 00:22:06.657
but into an investment account.

00:22:06.657 --> 00:22:07.679
- Into an investment account,

00:22:07.679 --> 00:22:08.529
where to put that money,

00:22:08.529 --> 00:22:09.520
then we teach you the ways to do that.

00:22:09.520 --> 00:22:10.994
It's got to be diversified.
- Yeah.

00:22:10.994 --> 00:22:12.160
- Never put all your money in the market.

00:22:12.160 --> 00:22:13.660
- I want to, okay.

